## 3. Analysis

### Stellar mass and star formation rate

We use the photometric redshift code z-PEG (Le Borgne & Rocca-Volmerange, 2002), which is based on the spectral synthesis code PEGASE.2 (Fioc & Rocca-Volmerange, 1997), to estimate the host-galaxy stellar mass (M_{\\rm stellar}) and star-formation rate (SFR). z-PEG fits the observed galaxy colors with galaxy SED templates corresponding to 9 spectral types (SB, Im, Sd, Sc, Sbc, Sb, Sa, S0 and E). We assume a Salpeter (1955) initial-mass function (IMF). The photometry is corrected for foreground Milky Way reddening of E(B-V)=0.109 mag (Schlafy & Finkbeiner, 2011; Shappee et al., 2017) with R_{V}=3.1 and a Cardelli, Clayton, & Mathis (1989, CCM) reddening law.

Using our 14-band photometry (see Section 2), we measure a host M_{\\rm stellar} of \\log(M/M_{\\odot})=10.49^{+0.08}_{-0.20}, corresponding to a halo mass of \\log(M_{\\rm halo}/M_{\\odot})=11.96 using the M_{\\rm stellar}-M_{\\rm halo} relation derived in Yang et al. (2008), assuming \\log(M_{0}/M_{\\odot})=9.8, \\log(M_{\\rm h}/M_{\\odot})=10.7, \\alpha=0.6 and \\beta=2.9 in their Eq. (7). The observed photometry and best-fit template can be found in Fig. 3.

In Fig. 4 we compare the measured M_{\\rm stellar} to that for the host galaxies of supernovae (SNe) and both short and long GRBs. Similar to SNe Ia and core-collapse SNe, sGRBs can be found in galaxies with a wide range of M_{\\rm stellar}. By contrast, long GRBs are predominantly found in low-mass galaxies. We find that NGC 4993 is

